  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Tuesday)\nIssued at 157 PM CDT WED SEP 30 2026\n\nThe warming trend will persist through Friday. While humidity\nlevels will remain relatively low, they will be higher than those\nobserved this afternoon as moisture begins advecting from the\nGulf. Positioned along the ridge, Central Alabama will experience\nsouth to southeasterly low-level flow through the remainder of\nthe week, supporting onshore moisture return and steadily\nincreasing dew points. Although moisture must recover further\nbefore significant precipitation can occur, low rain chances\nreturn late Thursday afternoon across the southern counties.\n\nMore substantial rain chances are delayed until late this weekend\nas an approaching frontal system slows. Consequently, the highest\nprecipitation chances are expected Saturday night through Sunday.\nWPC has placed the area in a 5 to 15% risk of exceeding flash\nflood criteria late Saturday through Sunday as a 250mb jet streak\nslides to our north and puts northwest Alabama in the entrance\nside of the jet.\n\nConfidence is medium-to-high that the front will stall or become\nslow-moving, keeping rain chances elevated into early next week.\nOn Sunday, the proximity of the front will help usher in more\nGulf moisture as a weak shortwave slides into southern Alabama\nahead of the front. Total rainfall amounts of 1 to 2 inches are\nmost likely across much of Central Alabama between Friday and\nMonday, though locally higher amounts remain possible. Increased\ncloud cover and widespread precipitation will moderate\ntemperatures over the weekend. Guidance suggests sufficient upper-\nlevel support will eventually push the surface boundary through\non Monday, ushering in cooler and drier conditions for the first\nhalf of next week.\n\n16",
